DEEP[1]=('Chapter 7 is named Jñāna-Vijñāna Yoga, the yoga of knowledge and realisation. It opens the middle six chapters, where Kṛṣṇa speaks of his own nature. Arjuna asked no question to prompt it: Kṛṣṇa continues from 6.47, where the devoted yogi was called the best.',
 'Commentators note <i>āsakta</i>, attached: the Gītā has warned against attachment to objects, but attachment to the Lord is praised. Śaṅkara reads <i>samagram</i>, “in full”, as knowing the Lord with all his powers and glories. Rāmānuja reads it as knowing his nature, qualities and manifestations. The chapter unfolds what is to be known: his lower and higher natures, his presence in all things, and who comes to him.',
 'Attachment to what is highest frees rather than binds. Let your deepest attachment be to what is truly worthy of it.')
DEEP[2]=('',
 'The Chāndogya and Muṇḍaka Upaniṣads ask about the knowledge by which everything else becomes known. Muṇḍaka 1.1.3 asks: <i>kasmin nu bhagavo vijñāte sarvam idaṃ vijñātaṃ bhavati?</i>, “Sir, by knowing what does all this become known?”. Commentators see 7.2 as the Gītā’s answer. Śaṅkara defines <i>jñāna</i> as knowledge of the Self from scripture and <i>vijñāna</i> as its direct experience. Rāmānuja takes <i>jñāna</i> as knowledge of the Lord’s nature and <i>vijñāna</i> as knowledge of his distinguishing qualities. The promise is bold: nothing more will remain to be known.',
 'Some knowledge opens all other doors. Look for the root, not just the branches.')
DEEP[3]=('',
 'Commentators find this verse both humbling and inspiring. Śaṅkara says it shows how rare and precious this knowledge is, to encourage the seeker. Rāmānuja says even among the successful yogis who know the self, few know the Lord fully. The verse is often quoted to explain why spiritual teachers are rare. Some modern readers find it discouraging; teachers answer that rarity does not mean impossibility. 7.19 says the wise one comes after many births, and 6.45 promises that effort accumulates.',
 'Something rare is not out of reach; it is precious. Let that make you more careful, not less hopeful.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 'The five elements, <i>pañca-bhūta</i>, plus mind, intellect and ego make eight. Commentators note that the Sāṅkhya system lists twenty-four principles of matter; this verse names their main headings. Śaṅkara reads each as standing for a group: earth for its subtle form, mind for its source, and so on. Rāmānuja says these eight are the Lord’s material nature, his body. The striking point is that mind and ego are counted as matter, not self. The thinking, planning, “I”-making part of us belongs to nature. The true self is something else.',
 'Your thoughts and moods are real, but they are not the whole of you. There is a knower behind them.')
DEEP[5]=('',
 '<i>Parā prakṛti</i>, higher nature, is consciousness, the life in beings. Commentators differ on how it relates to the Lord. Śaṅkara: it is the knower, the conscious principle that appears as many individual selves through ignorance. Rāmānuja: it is the totality of individual selves, real and many, forming part of the Lord’s body. Madhva: the selves are eternally distinct from the Lord and depend on him. All agree that consciousness is higher than matter, and that the world is “upheld” by it: without life, matter would have no meaning.',
 'Every living being carries the higher nature, life itself. That is reason enough for reverence.')
DEEP[6]=('',
 'Commentators see the Lord as both the material cause (what the world is made of) and the efficient cause (who makes it). The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (1.1.7) gives the image of a spider spinning its web from itself and drawing it back. Śaṅkara says the Lord, through his māyā, is the cause of all. Rāmānuja says the Lord, with matter and selves as his body, transforms into the world while remaining himself. The words <i>prabhava</i>, origin, and <i>pralaya</i>, dissolution, return in 9.18 and 10.8. The world comes from one source and returns to it.',
 'If everything comes from one source, then everything is related. That changes how we treat the world.')
DEEP[7]=('',
 'The thread image appears in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (3.7), where Uddālaka asks Yājñavalkya about the <i>sūtra</i>, the thread by which this world and the next are tied together, and the inner controller. Commentators of every school love the Gītā’s version. Śaṅkara: the Lord is the reality that runs through all appearances. Rāmānuja: the Lord is the inner support of all beings, as the thread supports the pearls. Madhva: the Lord holds all in being. The thread is hidden; the pearls are seen. Without the thread, they scatter.',
 'Look for the thread in your own life: what holds your days, relationships and work together?')
DEEP[8]=('',
 'Verses 8–11 name the Lord in the essence of each thing. Commentators note the pattern from the Sāṅkhya elements: water’s quality is taste, fire’s is light, space’s is sound, earth’s is smell (7.9). Śaṅkara says the Lord is the essence that makes each thing what it is. Rāmānuja says the Lord is the inner controller of each quality. <i>Praṇava</i>, Oṃ, is called the essence of the Vedas; the Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad is devoted to it. <i>Pauruṣa</i>, manliness, is read as courage, energy and capacity in people. This list anticipates the longer list of chapter 10.',
 'Taste your water slowly once today, as a reminder. The extraordinary often hides in the ordinary.')
DEEP[9]=('',
 'Commentators note that the Lord is the pleasant smell of earth, not the foul; the best, not the worst, of each thing. Śaṅkara says “pure” means natural, unmixed. Many readers think of the smell of rain on dry soil, which Indian poets call <i>mṛd-gandha</i>. <i>Jīvana</i>, life, is the Lord in all beings. <i>Tapas</i>, austerity, is the Lord in the disciplined. The list moves from elements to living beings to spiritual effort. The Divine is in the senses’ best experiences and in the soul’s highest efforts.',
 'Next time you smell rain on earth, pause. Let it be a moment of quiet wonder.')
DEEP[10]=('',
 '<i>Bīja</i>, seed, returns in 9.18 and 10.39: the Lord as the imperishable seed. Commentators note the image: the seed contains the whole tree in potential, and remains when the tree is gone. Śaṅkara says the Lord is the cause of all, as the seed of a tree. Rāmānuja says the Lord is the one cause that persists through all changes. <i>Buddhi</i>, intelligence, and <i>tejas</i>, splendour or brilliance, are gifts to be used with humility; they are his, not ours. Teachers often apply this verse to talent: honour its source.',
 'Your intelligence and gifts are not just yours. Use them gratefully, as things on loan.')
DEEP[11]=('',
 'This verse balances 3.37, where desire was called the great enemy. Commentators explain: desire against dharma binds; desire within dharma is sacred. Śaṅkara gives examples: desire for food enough to live, for a family within marriage. Rāmānuja says the Lord is present in desires that serve right ends. The Hindu tradition names four aims of life: <i>dharma</i> (right living), <i>artha</i> (wealth), <i>kāma</i> (pleasure) and <i>mokṣa</i> (freedom). Kāma has a place when guided by dharma. The strength that is divine is strength without selfish passion, used to protect.',
 'Not all wanting is wrong. Wanting what is good, within right limits, is part of a healthy life.')
DEEP[12]=('',
 'Commentators find this verse subtle. All three guṇas come from the Lord, yet he is not contained or controlled by them. Śaṅkara says the guṇas depend on the Lord as a dream depends on the dreamer; the dreamer is not in the dream. Rāmānuja says the guṇas exist in the Lord as part of his body but do not affect his nature. The image of the sky and clouds is often used: clouds are in the sky, but the sky is not in the clouds and is not stained by them. 9.4–5 develops the same paradox.',
 'Moods come from somewhere, but you are not your moods. Something in you holds them without being them.')
DEEP[13]=('',
 'Commentators note that the world is not bad; it is distracting. We are caught up in the play of the guṇas: moods, events, attractions, fears. So we miss the one behind them. Śaṅkara says the guṇas create the delusion that the body and mind are the Self. Rāmānuja says beings, absorbed in the pleasures and pains of the guṇas, do not turn to the Lord, whose joy is far greater. The verse explains the rarity of 7.3: it is not that the Lord is hidden, but that our attention is elsewhere.',
 'Step back from the day’s events for a few minutes and ask: what is behind all this?')
DEEP[14]=('',
 '<i>Māyā</i> here is the Lord’s divine power that creates and veils. Commentators of every school treat this verse as central. Rāmānuja calls it the key verse for <i>prapatti</i>, surrender: no one can cross māyā by their own effort; only by taking refuge in the Lord. Śaṅkara says those who take refuge in the Lord as their Self cross ignorance by knowledge. Madhva says the Lord’s grace alone frees. The image is of a vast river: we cannot swim across, but a boat is offered. The verse is chanted widely in devotional traditions.',
 'Some knots can’t be untied by our hands alone. Asking for help, from God or from others, is wisdom.')
DEEP[15]=('',
 'Commentators name four types: <i>duṣkṛtin</i>, evil-doers; <i>mūḍha</i>, deluded fools; <i>narādhama</i>, “lowest of men”, those who know but do not act; and those of <i>āsura bhāva</i>, demonic nature, who actively oppose the good. Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja both stress that these are states, not permanent identities. The Gītā elsewhere (9.30–31) says even the worst wrongdoer who turns to the Lord becomes righteous. Chapter 16 describes the āsurika nature in detail. The verse explains why some turn away, without closing the door to change.',
 'No one is fixed in a state forever. Even long habits can turn, with a single sincere choice.')
DEEP[16]=('',
 'Commentators give traditional examples. <i>Ārta</i>, the distressed: Draupadī in the dice hall, or Gajendra the elephant caught by a crocodile, who called on Viṣṇu. <i>Jijñāsu</i>, the inquirer: Uddhava, who asked Kṛṣṇa to teach him. <i>Arthārthī</i>, the seeker of wealth or position: Dhruva, the boy who wanted a kingdom and ended up with God. <i>Jñānī</i>, the wise: Śuka, son of Vyāsa, or Prahlāda. All four are called <i>sukṛtin</i>, virtuous. Turning to God for any reason is good, because it begins a relationship.',
 'Whatever brings you to prayer is a good beginning. Many who came for help stayed for love.')
DEEP[17]=('',
 '<i>Eka-bhakti</i>, devotion to the One alone. Commentators explain why the wise one excels: the others want something from God; the wise one wants God. Śaṅkara says the wise one sees the Lord as his own Self, so there is nothing else to want. Rāmānuja says the wise one loves the Lord for his own sake and cannot live without him. The mutual dearness, <i>priyo’ham sa ca mama priyaḥ</i>, is one of the Gītā’s most touching lines. It recalls 6.30: “I am never lost to him, nor he to me”.',
 'The purest love wants nothing in return. Try loving someone today without expecting anything back.')
DEEP[18]=('',
 '<i>Udāra</i>, noble or generous: Kṛṣṇa praises all four kinds of devotees, even those who come for wealth. Commentators find this generous. <i>Ātmaiva me</i>, “my very Self”, is read differently. Śaṅkara: literally, the knower is one with the Lord. Rāmānuja: “as dear to me as my own self”; the Lord cannot live without such a devotee. Madhva: the knower is closest to the Lord. The verse shows the Gītā’s typical spirit: it ranks, but it does not reject.',
 'Don’t look down on anyone’s reasons for seeking. Every sincere step toward the good is noble.')
DEEP[19]=('',
 '<i>Vāsudevaḥ sarvam</i> is one of the best-loved phrases in Vaiṣṇava devotion. <i>Vāsudeva</i>, son of Vasudeva, is Kṛṣṇa; the name is also read as “the one who dwells in all”. Commentators note the arc: many births of effort lead to one realisation. Śaṅkara reads it as the knowledge that the Self of all is Vāsudeva. Rāmānuja reads it as the knowledge that all beings are the Lord’s body and depend on him. The Viṣṇu Purāṇa and Bhāgavata Purāṇa praise this vision. Gandhi was fond of the word <i>mahātmā</i> in its original sense: one whose soul is great, not one with titles.',
 'The goal is to see the Divine in all. Every kind glance at the world is practice for it.')
DEEP[20]=('',
 'Commentators note the tone: not condemnation, but observation. People worship different deities for different wishes, according to their temperament and the desires that move them. Śaṅkara says desire narrows their vision. Rāmānuja says such worship is valid but limited. Hindu practice has many deities for particular blessings: Gaṇeśa for removing obstacles, Lakṣmī for prosperity, Sarasvatī for learning. The Gītā does not forbid these; it points beyond them, as the next verses show.',
 'Notice what you most often pray or wish for. It reveals what you value most right now.')
DEEP[21]=('',
 'This is one of the Gītā’s most generous verses. Commentators say the Lord does not compete with other gods; he strengthens every sincere faith. Śaṅkara says the Lord as inner controller makes each person’s faith steady. Rāmānuja says the other deities are the Lord’s body; worship of them reaches him in the end. Ramakrishna, who practised several religious paths, often cited such verses. The verse supports the Indian tradition of respecting many forms of worship. It also reminds the devotee that his own faith is a gift.',
 'Respect others’ sincere faith. The same Source may be steadying it, as it steadies yours.')
DEEP[22]=('',
 'Commentators say this verse reveals the hidden giver behind every blessing. Śaṅkara: the Lord, as the one who knows all actions and their fruits, gives every reward. Rāmānuja: the deities are part of the Lord’s body, and their power comes from him. Madhva: the Lord gives through the deities, who depend on him. The idea returns in 9.23–24, where the Lord says that those who worship other gods worship him, though not in the right way, and that he is the enjoyer and lord of all sacrifices.',
 'Behind every gift there is a giver. Gratitude for one blessing can lead us to the Source of all.')
DEEP[23]=('',
 'Commentators explain: what one worships determines where one goes. The heavens of the gods are real but temporary; when merit ends, one returns (9.21). The Lord is the eternal goal. Śaṅkara says those who worship for limited fruits get limited results, though the effort is the same. Rāmānuja notes the irony: the same effort could win the highest. The verse does not mock other worship; it calls it <i>alpa-medhas</i>, of small vision, and invites a larger one.',
 'Aim at the highest you can imagine. The effort is similar; the reward is far greater.')
DEEP[24]=('',
 'Commentators read this verse in two main ways. Śaṅkara: the unwise think the formless Lord has taken on a body like ours, through karma; they do not know his true nature as the Self. Rāmānuja and Madhva: the unwise think Kṛṣṇa is an ordinary man who has become prominent; they do not know that his form is divine and eternal. Both readings warn against reducing the Divine to the merely human. Many in the Mahābhārata saw Kṛṣṇa as a clever prince; few, like Bhīṣma and Vidura, recognised him.',
 'People are often more than they seem. Look again at those you think you already know.')
DEEP[25]=('',
 '<i>Yoga-māyā</i> is the Lord’s power to veil and reveal himself. In the Bhāgavata Purāṇa, Yogamāyā is also a goddess, the Lord’s power personified, who helped in Kṛṣṇa’s birth. Commentators explain that the veil is not cruel; it protects the freedom of beings and the intimacy of devotion. Śaṅkara says the veil is the ignorance born of the guṇas. Rāmānuja says the Lord’s human form itself veils his divinity from the unready. The veil lifts for those who seek with love, as for Arjuna in chapter 11.',
 'What is hidden can be found by those who look sincerely. Keep looking.')
DEEP[26]=('',
 'Commentators note the asymmetry: the Lord knows all; no one knows him, except by his grace. Śaṅkara adds “except the devotee who takes refuge in me”. Rāmānuja says no one knows him by their own power. The verse links back to 4.5, where Kṛṣṇa says he remembers all past births. Some readers find comfort here: we are fully known, even when we do not know ourselves. The Psalms say something similar: “O Lord, thou hast searched me, and known me” (Psalm 139).',
 'You are fully known, even in what you hide from yourself. That can be a comfort rather than a fear.')
DEEP[27]=('',
 '<i>Sarge</i>, at birth or at creation. Commentators say every being is born with likes and dislikes, the basic programme of survival. These create the delusion of opposites: pleasant and painful, mine and not mine. Śaṅkara says this delusion hides the Self. Rāmānuja says beings are born with impressions from past lives that produce attraction and aversion. Patañjali names <i>rāga</i> and <i>dveṣa</i> as afflictions (Yoga Sūtra 2.7–8). The verse explains why spiritual life feels like swimming upstream: we start with a strong current of likes and dislikes.',
 'Your likes and dislikes are real, but they are not the whole truth about the world. Hold them a little more loosely.')
DEEP[28]=('',
 'Commentators see a sequence: good deeds clear away sin; a clear mind sees past opposites; such a mind can love steadily. Śaṅkara says purity of mind from good action leads to steady devotion. Rāmānuja says the end of sin removes the obstacles to worship. <i>Dṛḍha-vrata</i>, “firm in vows”, describes devotion that does not depend on mood. The verse encourages karma yoga as the ground of bhakti: good action prepares the heart for love.',
 'Keep doing good, even when you don’t feel inspired. It prepares the ground for something deeper.')
DEEP[29]=('',
 'Commentators note the motive: freedom from old age and death, the oldest human longing. Śaṅkara says such seekers come to know Brahman, the inner Self (<i>adhyātma</i>) and all action. Rāmānuja reads this as a description of the seeker of self-realisation, who knows the self’s nature as distinct from matter. The technical terms, <i>brahma</i>, <i>adhyātma</i>, <i>karma</i>, and those of 7.30, prompt Arjuna’s questions at the start of chapter 8. The Gītā builds its structure through such threads.',
 'Fear of ageing and death can be the beginning of a deeper search. Let it lead you inward.')
DEEP[30]=('',
 'Three more technical terms: <i>adhibhūta</i>, the Lord as the basis of material beings; <i>adhidaiva</i>, as the basis of the gods or cosmic powers; <i>adhiyajña</i>, as the Lord of sacrifice. Chapter 8 (8.3–4) defines each. Commentators note the phrase <i>prayāṇa-kāle</i>, “at the time of departure”: knowledge gained in life holds firm at death. This prepares the main theme of chapter 8, what happens at the end. Śaṅkara says the knower remembers the Lord even in the confusion of dying. Rāmānuja says such remembrance at death leads to the Lord. The colophon names chapter 7 Jñāna-Vijñāna Yoga.',
 'What we practise daily is what remains when everything else falls away. Practise remembering what matters.')
